title: Prefab copies keep their own changes
intro: A copy can differ from its prefab. Griff marks what it changed, keeps it when the prefab is saved, and lets you revert it or apply it to the prefab.
project: Platformer | Prefab Overrides

chapter: Three gems from one prefab | Entity > Save as Prefab
look: 8, -2, 4
deselect
create: Sprite
rename: Gem
set: Transform.Position = 5, -3, 0
set: Sprite.Tint = #29ADFF
set: Sprite.Size = 0.6, 0.6
add: Pickup
menu: Entity > Save as Prefab
expectprefab: Gem = Gem
key: Ctrl+D
rename: Gem 2
set: Transform.Position = 7.5, -3, 0
key: Ctrl+D
rename: Gem 3
set: Transform.Position = 10, -3, 0
expectoverrides: Gem 3 = none
say: Three gems, all copies of one prefab. Where each one is placed is its own, so none of them changes anything yet.

chapter: Change one copy | The Inspector marks what it changed
select: Gem 2
set: Sprite.Tint = #FF004D
expectoverrides: Gem 2 = Sprite.Tint
explain: override:Sprite.Tint | A changed field | Making this one gem red marks its tint as a change from the prefab: a mark by the field, and its name in bold. Its tooltip says what the prefab has.
explain: prefabnote | Copy of the Gem prefab, with 1 change | And the note at the top counts what this copy changes.

chapter: Save the prefab | Each copy keeps what it changed
select: Gem 3
set: Sprite.Size = 0.9, 0.9
set: Pickup.Amount = 5
say: Now say gems should be bigger and worth five. We change another gem and save it over the prefab.
menu: Entity > Save as Prefab
answer: Yes
expectvalue: Gem 2.Pickup.Amount = 5
expectvalue: Gem 2.Sprite.Tint = #FF004D
expectvalue: Gem.Sprite.Tint = #29ADFF
expectvalue: Gem.Pickup.Amount = 5
explain: world:4, -1.5, 12, -4.2 | Updated, changes kept | Every copy takes the new size and value. The red gem stays red, because that was its own change. Copies in other scenes keep theirs too.

chapter: Revert or apply | Click the mark by a changed field
select: Gem
set: Pickup.Amount = 2
expectoverrides: Gem = Pickup.Amount
revertfield: Pickup.Amount
expectvalue: Gem.Pickup.Amount = 5
expectoverrides: Gem = none
say: Revert to Prefab puts a field back to the prefab's value.
select: Gem 2
applyfield: Sprite.Tint
expectvalue: Gem.Sprite.Tint = #FF004D
expectvalue: Gem 3.Sprite.Tint = #FF004D
expectoverrides: Gem 2 = none
explain: world:4, -1.5, 12, -4.2 | Apply to Prefab | Apply to Prefab goes the other way: red becomes the prefab's colour, so every gem that hadn't changed its colour turns red too.

chapter: Put a whole copy back | Entity > Revert to Prefab
select: Gem 3
set: Sprite.Tint = #FFA300
add: Light
expectoverrides: Gem 3 = Sprite.Tint, added Light
explain: component:Light | Added to this copy | A component a copy adds is marked Added, and it stays when the prefab is saved.
menu: Entity > Revert to Prefab
expectoverrides: Gem 3 = none
expectvalue: Gem 3.Sprite.Tint = #FF004D
say: Revert to Prefab on the Entity menu undoes everything a copy changed, in one step, and leaves it where it was placed.
